You're right that there is only one flag, "oerodeproof", which means
the object is immune to all kinds of damage; but I just tested the
oilskin sack in lava. It burns. The oilskin sack isn't actually
erodeproof. The protection of it's contents is done by checking the
object type directly.

Well anyway. I've sorted out those lava problems now. Nethack's code
is a bit of a mess. Those lava bugs (and many other lava bugs) were
part of the unmodded code; but as it turns out there is already a
function in the unmodded code, called fire_damage, which required
relatively little modification to do exactly what lava should do,
without the bugs... and this fire_damage function is what the game
already uses for handling objects thrown into lava, or even monsters
walking into lava. Just not the player, for some unknown reason.

I've just spent some time tinkering with the fire_damage function,
shifting bits of code around and such, and I've noticed some strange
behavior... As it turns out, you can wish for anything to be
fireproof, and it will be, but it only displays the words "fireproof"
on somethings. Furthermore, the only the fireproofness of weapons is
checked when merging stacks of stuff. So for example, you can wish for
1 blessed fixed tallow candle, identify it, then wish for 1 blessed
tallow candle (not fixed), and identify that... so that you have 1
fireproof candle and one not fireproof. then drop the unfixed candle,
and pick it up. Now you have 2 fireproof candles! The same thing
happens with food and scrolls etc, but those don't display the
"fireproof" text. I think I'll leave that bug alone, but I've made
enchant weapon only erodeproof non-stackable items (and all weapons).
I'm not sure that "fireproof candles" makes physical sense anyway.
Maybe it would be better to have some different way to erodeproof non-
weapons, but this is good enough for now.


